-- statement:
--   One half of **Theorem 1.1 of Bux–Kassmann–Schulze**: the inequality
--   $\mathcal E[k,f] \lesssim |f|^2_{H^{\alpha/2}}$, which the source obtains directly from the
--   *upper* bound of assumption (2) and describes as trivially true — the comparability of the two
--   forms is interesting only in the other direction.
--
--   Given a kernel $k$ bounded above by $\Lambda \lVert x-y\rVert^{-d-\alpha}$, the energy
--   $\mathcal E[k,f] = \iint |f(x)-f(y)|^2 k(x,y)$ is bounded by $\Lambda$ times the Gagliardo
--   energy, pointwise under the integral sign. No cone geometry, no configuration and no lower bound
--   on $k$ enter; that is the whole content, and the reason the source spends no words on it.
--
--   The substantive direction — the Gagliardo energy controlled by $\mathcal E[k,f]$ — is what
--   Sections 3 to 5 are for, and it is the mission's open frontier in dimensions above two. See the
--   Theorem 1.1 milestone.
